{
  "schema": "cooperative_group_sharing_residency_v2",
  "mechanism": "cooperative_group_sharing_sharer_count_and_step_arithmetic",
  "hardware_id": "metax_c500",
  "allowed_use": "Evidence for how far operand sharing between co-scheduled programs scales with the number of sharers, with the dispatch spacing between them, with a second stream that is itself reused, and with the arithmetic a program spends on each k step.  The controls at group size one reproduce the no-sharing baseline and tie the single-stream and two-stream kernels to each other.  It also carries the negative result that an operand's row placement does not change realised sharing unless the placement is a fixed stride, which is an aliasing effect rather than a property of gathering.",
  "forbidden_use": "These realised-sharing values must not price any production traffic.  Measured at the production grouped-GEMM operating point -- ninety activation sharers one GROUP_SIZE_M of eight apart, eight adjacent weight sharers, both streams reused -- this probe realises about four times the sharing the production kernel realises, so carrying these numbers into a Forward path would under-predict DRAM traffic by that factor.  They bound the mechanism from above; they do not describe it.",
  "production_or_evaluation_duration_consumed": false,
  "instrument": "mcProfiler perf_exec --custom --per-kernel, one marker window, one numbered per-invocation file per ordinal",
  "counters": [
    "Global Memory Read bytes",
    "L2C Hit Rate"
  ],
  "geometry": {
    "programs": 8192,
    "block_threads": 256,
    "slice_bytes": 4096,
    "steps": 64,
    "logical_bytes_per_program": 524288,
    "note": "Every configuration reads identical logical bytes per program in both streams and differs only in the sharer count, the dispatch spacing, whether the second stream is shared, and the arithmetic per step.  On the sharer-count axis the shared footprint falls as one over the group size because the per-program byte count is held fixed; both ends sit two orders of magnitude above L2, so capacity is not the operative variable here, but this is not a strict equal-footprint control and the reading holds only far above L2."
  },
  "why_independent_axes": "Every earlier run of this probe fixed the group at eight sharers, so its headline -- sharing realised almost in full at spacing one -- was never checked at the sharer count a production launch has.  A grouped GEMM with GROUP_SIZE_M of one shares its activation tile between num_pid_n adjacent programs, ninety for the shape under study.  The sharer count, the second reused stream and the arithmetic per step are read off the launch and the kernel source, never off a prediction error.",
  "finding": "Adjacent sharing is exact up to sixteen sharers (1.00, 2.00, 4.00, 8.00, 16.00 of ideal) and then decays gently: 28.63 of 32, 51.83 of 64, 59.93 of 90, 82.73 of 128 and 118.68 of 256.  It does not saturate at a small constant.  At ninety sharers it decays with dispatch spacing as 62.52, 44.00, 27.67, 16.23 and 5.83 for spacings one, two, four, eight and sixteen.  Repeats agree to three figures (group eight: 8.00 twice; group ninety at spacing one: 62.52 twice).  Giving the second stream its own group and spacing, so both streams are reused as a grouped GEMM's operands are, does not destroy sharing: the production structure reads 0.080 of its logical bytes, slightly below the 0.093 an independent composition of the two single-stream measurements predicts, so the two reused streams help rather than evict each other.  Driving the kernel from pure load to heavily compute bound -- dependent FMAs per thread per step from zero to 4096, which raises compute instructions 47 times and total cycles 8.2 times at constant memory instructions -- moves the same figure only from 0.080 to 0.072.  Sharing here is therefore neither capacity limited (it survives a reuse distance 45 times L2), nor limited by mutual eviction between reused streams, nor limited by the phase drift that arithmetic per step induces.  The activation stream was then rebuilt as the gather it actually is -- 64 rows of BK * element_bytes per k step, identical byte and line count to the slice, only the row placement differing -- under three layouts that are all bijections over the same slots.  Rows laid consecutively read 0.077 of logical bytes and rows placed by a genuine shuffled permutation read 0.077, indistinguishable over three interleaved repeats each.  A transposed map, which spaces an object's rows by a fixed 384 KiB slot stride, reads 0.141 instead; that collapse is set aliasing from the fixed stride, not the gather, and it disappears the moment the same scatter is produced by a permutation.  Gathering an operand therefore costs no sharing on this target, and a production scatter, which routing makes irregular, carries no such penalty.  Finally the shared working set was scaled at a fixed sharing structure: the dispatch width from 8192 to 65536 programs multiplies the number of distinct shared objects in proportion, from 24 MiB to 184 MiB, while the sharers of one object stay ninety and stay eight apart and every per-program byte is unchanged.  The realised sharing is flat across that range, about 30, and the ratio stays at 0.079.  Co-scheduled sharing on this target is therefore independent of how much shared data the kernel touches in total.",
  "build": "/opt/maca/mxgpu_llvm/bin/mxcc -x maca -offload-arch native --maca-path=/opt/maca -O3 -o probe cooperative_group_sharing_residency.cpp",
